1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,470
Chai Corner is ready to
open its sixth outlet.

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:06,153
But the city has four thousand streets.

3
00:00:06,153 --> 00:00:07,882
Which one should Ravi pick?

4
00:00:08,432 --> 00:00:11,835
Last time, the rational model
said, check every option.

5
00:00:12,135 --> 00:00:14,795
But checking every street
would take a whole year.

6
00:00:15,095 --> 00:00:17,692
By then, the best shops
are already rented.

7
00:00:18,342 --> 00:00:19,868
Perfect takes too long.

8
00:00:19,868 --> 00:00:21,394
Ravi needs good enough.

9
00:00:22,094 --> 00:00:25,500
This idea comes from Herbert
Simon, an American thinker.

10
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,284
He studied how people in
organisations really decide.

11
00:00:29,634 --> 00:00:34,062
In 1947, he wrote the book,
Administrative Behavior.

12
00:00:34,412 --> 00:00:40,574
And in 1978, he won the Nobel Prize in
Economics, for his research on decision

13
00:00:40,574 --> 00:00:42,563
making in organisations.

14
00:00:43,213 --> 00:00:45,364
So, what is bounded rationality?

15
00:00:45,664 --> 00:00:49,974
Bounded rationality means people try
to be rational, but their thinking

16
00:00:49,974 --> 00:00:51,645
has limits, or bounds.

17
00:00:52,145 --> 00:00:53,836
A bound is like a fence.

18
00:00:53,836 --> 00:00:56,527
You can think freely, but only inside it.

19
00:00:57,177 --> 00:00:59,317
Every decision has three bounds.

20
00:00:59,667 --> 00:01:00,567
One.

21
00:01:00,215 --> 00:01:01,846
Incomplete information.

22
00:01:01,846 --> 00:01:04,047
Ravi cannot know every street.

23
00:01:04,397 --> 00:01:05,297
Two.

24
00:01:04,959 --> 00:01:06,083
Limited time.

25
00:01:06,083 --> 00:01:08,583
A good shop gets rented in days.

26
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:09,833
Three.

27
00:01:09,582 --> 00:01:11,105
Limited brain power.

28
00:01:11,105 --> 00:01:14,873
Nobody can compare four
thousand options in their head.

29
00:01:15,523 --> 00:01:17,213
So what do people do?

30
00:01:17,213 --> 00:01:18,395
They satisfice.

31
00:01:18,745 --> 00:01:20,920
Satisfice is Simon's own word.

32
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:23,931
It joins two words, satisfy and suffice.

33
00:01:24,281 --> 00:01:28,251
It means choosing the first option that
is good enough, instead of searching

34
00:01:28,251 --> 00:01:29,502
for the perfect one.

35
00:01:30,152 --> 00:01:31,381
Watch Ravi do it.

36
00:01:31,731 --> 00:01:33,606
First, he sets three must haves.

37
00:01:33,956 --> 00:01:35,889
Lots of people walking by.

38
00:01:35,889 --> 00:01:38,731
Rent under fifty thousand rupees a month.

39
00:01:38,731 --> 00:01:40,612
And no chai stall nearby.

40
00:01:40,962 --> 00:01:43,961
Then he searches one small
area, street by street.

41
00:01:44,311 --> 00:01:47,090
Street one is busy, but
the rent is too high.

42
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:50,870
Street two is cheap, but there
is a chai stall next door.

43
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:53,550
Street three passes all three checks.

44
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:56,035
Ravi stops, and signs that week.

45
00:01:56,035 --> 00:01:58,585
Three streets checked, not four thousand.

46
00:01:59,235 --> 00:02:00,909
Compare the two models.

47
00:02:00,909 --> 00:02:02,890
The rational model optimises.

48
00:02:02,890 --> 00:02:06,720
It assumes every option is
known, and time is unlimited.

49
00:02:07,070 --> 00:02:08,968
Bounded rationality satisfices.

50
00:02:08,968 --> 00:02:12,121
It accepts the limits,
and stops at good enough.

51
00:02:12,771 --> 00:02:14,892
So where do you see this at work today?

52
00:02:15,242 --> 00:02:16,257
Must haves.

53
00:02:16,257 --> 00:02:20,867
A hiring team lists its must
haves before it reads a single CV.

54
00:02:21,217 --> 00:02:22,596
A small search area.

55
00:02:22,596 --> 00:02:26,789
A shop owner looks for suppliers
in one city, not the whole country.

56
00:02:27,139 --> 00:02:29,293
Rules of thumb, quick shortcuts.

57
00:02:29,293 --> 00:02:33,475
A restaurant orders what it sold
last Friday, plus a little extra.

58
00:02:33,825 --> 00:02:34,752
A deadline.

59
00:02:34,752 --> 00:02:38,703
A team agrees to decide by
Friday, with the facts it has.

60
00:02:39,053 --> 00:02:40,402
Stop at good enough.

61
00:02:40,402 --> 00:02:45,092
A buyer orders from the first supplier
that meets price, quality, and delivery.

62
00:02:45,742 --> 00:02:48,050
So is good enough always good enough?

63
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:50,267
The honest answer is, yes and no.

64
00:02:50,617 --> 00:02:51,702
The good side.

65
00:02:51,702 --> 00:02:55,861
It is fast, it saves money, and
it fits how real people think.

66
00:02:56,211 --> 00:02:57,306
The hard side.

67
00:02:57,306 --> 00:03:00,301
A better option might
be just one street away.

68
00:03:00,651 --> 00:03:04,234
And if your must haves are too
low, you settle for something poor.

69
00:03:04,584 --> 00:03:06,908
Simon's point was not to be lazy.

70
00:03:06,908 --> 00:03:09,847
Set smart must haves, then stop searching.

71
00:03:10,197 --> 00:03:11,777
So the lesson is balance.

72
00:03:11,777 --> 00:03:15,436
Aim high with your must haves,
then let good enough be enough.

73
00:03:16,086 --> 00:03:16,989
Quick check.

74
00:03:16,989 --> 00:03:21,349
Asha picks the first cup supplier
that is cheap, strong, and on time.

75
00:03:21,349 --> 00:03:22,644
What is she doing?

76
00:03:24,544 --> 00:03:25,444
Satisficing!

77
00:03:25,363 --> 00:03:29,667
She took the first option that met
her must haves, and stopped searching.

78
00:03:30,267 --> 00:03:31,280
So, remember.

79
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:36,296
Bounded rationality means our decisions
are limited by information, time,

80
00:03:36,296 --> 00:03:37,585
and brain power.

81
00:03:37,935 --> 00:03:41,438
So we satisfice, and pick the
first option that is good enough.

82
00:03:41,788 --> 00:03:47,443
The idea comes from Herbert Simon,
who won the Nobel Prize in 1978.

83
00:03:48,043 --> 00:03:52,538
Next time, Ravi learns to share his
work, with delegation of authority.
